Follow-up — concurrent consume timeout parity, 2026-10-13 01:23 UTC:
- The new paused-time regression initially failed because concurrent callbacks were wrapped in Tokio timeout and aborted at the Java consume-timeout threshold. Pinned Java `ConsumeMessageConcurrentlyService` waits for the listener, then classifies timeout only for a non-null status; thrown listener errors and null returns keep `EXCEPTION`/`RETURNNULL` priority.
- Concurrent callbacks now remain in flight until listener completion; timeout is recorded in the hook context after completion without changing the listener status or returned context. Both concurrent and orderly paths use the configured signed minute value directly, including Java-compatible millisecond overflow behavior. Existing task-drop cancellation remains in place for shutdown/caller cancellation.
- Updated the paused-time regression to assert the listener remains alive at the threshold, then returns successfully and is recorded as `TIME_OUT`. Initial behavior check failed as expected before the fix; focused check passes after the change.
- PASS: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer concurrent_consume_timeout --locked`; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer concurrent_listener_panic_is_isolated_and_retried --locked`; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer concurrent_listener_without_status_sends_fixture_back_for_retry --locked`; `cargo clippy -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings`; `cargo fmt --all`.
- Phase 16 remains open: bidirectional source/API/configuration and call-site audit, workspace-wide acceptance gates, matrix reconciliation and phase commit remain pending. Phases 17–18 have not started.

Follow-up — orderly consume timeout return priority, 2026-10-13 01:31 UTC:
- Re-read pinned Java `ConsumeMessageOrderlyService`: `RETURNNULL`/`EXCEPTION` are classified before the timeout condition, while non-null callback status may be classified `TIME_OUT`. Rust previously let timeout overwrite all orderly callback types. Restricted the override to callbacks with a returned status and added `orderly_timeout_preserves_java_null_and_exception_priority`.
- Rechecked POP against pinned `ConsumeMessagePopConcurrentlyService`; its `TIME_OUT` threshold is POP `invisibleTime`, which Rust already derives from the receipt. No POP code change was needed.
- PASS: `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer orderly_timeout_preserves_java_null_and_exception_priority --locked`; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer orderly_success_holds_until_commit_and_rollback_replays_in_order --locked`; full `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --locked` (152 unit, 19 Lite/POP, 3 offset, 1 orderly, 2 pull, 7 pull-result, 1 push-pipeline, 2 rebalance tests); strict consumer Clippy.
