Follow-up — TraceDispatcher lifecycle mapping and final local gates, 2026-10-13 01:47 UTC:
- Re-read pinned Java 5.5.1 `AsyncTraceDispatcher`: the end-transaction hook reads the host producer client ID; `hostConsumer` is assigned but has no reader in the client source. Rust creates the trace dispatcher under Producer/Push/Lite owners, derives the host identity from ClientConfig, and inherits the Lite host RPC hook. Java's process shutdown hook only flushes; Rust flushes on explicit owner shutdown and on trace-channel closure while Tokio is active.
- Added `dropping_trace_dispatcher_flushes_queued_data`, using a scripted Broker to verify queued trace delivery after the last dispatcher sender closes. Focused test PASS.
- The first workspace rerun exposed a TLS test-temp-directory collision (`AlreadyExists`) from PID-plus-sequence naming. Hardened the helper with a timestamp and collision retry; the focused TLS properties test PASS. A separate channel-observer test asserted cancellation synchronously even though shutdown permits its `spawn_blocking` worker to finish just after return; changed it to await cancellation with a one-second bound. Focused test PASS.
- Final gates PASS: `cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked`; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings`; `cargo fmt --all -- --check`; `mvn -q -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify` (32 scenarios); `python3 tests/differential/compare.py --self-test`; `python3 scripts/check-workspace-metadata.py`; `git diff --check`.
